// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// `(N-Store)` is owed of a collection LITERAL's elements too, and it was the one seam that
// said nothing.
//
// `(N-Dense)` says a `vector<τ>`'s elements are non-null unless the type is written
// `vector<τ?>`. The rule was enforced at the scalar seam and at the append seam, and nowhere
// inside a literal: `v: vector<integer> = [n]` with `n: integer?` stored the null and `v[0]`
// read back null, silently, on both backends and on the shipped 2026.8.0.
//
// It is the CURE the language itself names, which is what makes it worth its own guard rather
// than a footnote: loft#1223 refuses the bare `c += n` and sends the reader to `c += [n]` — the
// right spelling under @PLAN52's bracket rule, and until now the un-diagnosed one. Closing that
// issue moved the reader from warned to silent. A diagnostic must not go quiet along the path
// it recommends.
//
// One seam serves every shape. A typed local, a field assignment, a constructor field and a
// NESTED literal all reach `parse_item` with both types settled, so all four are cells here and
// none of them needed a check of its own.
//
// ⚠ WARNING, never an error — including at a NARROW width, where the shared
// `n_store_violation` would otherwise escalate because a `u8` slot spends all 256 values on
// real data and has no room for a null. The escalation is right about the slot and wrong about
// the moment: this seam was SILENT until now, so refusing here retro-breaks code that compiles
// today. Measured on the whole registry — `assets 0.2.0` writes `bp += [0 as u8?]`, whose value
// is never actually null, and the gate went from 42 pass to a COMPILE-BREAK. The narrow cell
// below is that shape, and it is here to hold the tier down: reporting where there was silence
// is a strict gain, refusing what a shipped package relies on is a break the freeze forbids,
// and raising the tier later is COMPATIBILITY.md's process rather than this seam's call.
//
// The quiet cells are load-bearing in the other direction: a `vector<τ?>` destination, a
// `null` into a `vector<S?>`, and a plain dense literal must stay silent, or the seam is
// warning about nullability rather than about `(N-Store)`.
//
// @falsified-at: f799213e — interpret warnings 0 -> 5 (measured by hand; see below)
//   `make falsify` reads INERT on BOTH backends here and that verdict is correct for what it
//   measures: its channels are exit / asserts / leak / panic / refusals, and this defect moves
//   none of them. On the parent the file compiles, runs, answers every value identically and
//   prints `1232 PASSED` — the whole defect is that nothing was SAID. A guard for a diagnostic
//   has no falsify channel, so the parent was run by hand with its own stdlib
//   (`<parent-build> --path <parent-tree> --interpret <this file>`): 0 of these warnings there,
//   5 here, with the run proven by its `1232 PASSED` line rather than by an empty grep.
//   Under the harness that difference IS a failure, because `@EXPECT_WARNING` scores both
//   directions — a missing warning fails the file exactly as an extra one does, which is also
//   what holds the quiet cells below quiet.
//
//   ARMED: none; the warning count is read by hand, or through the corpus runner's diagnostic
//   check.
//   HOLDS: every channel `falsify` scores — exit, asserts, leak, panic, refusals — is equal
//   on BOTH backends, so its INERT verdict is correct for what it measures. On the parent the
//   file compiles, runs and answers every value identically; what moves is the WARNING COUNT,
//   0 -> 5 on interpret. No falsify signature can confirm or deny this guard.
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into element 0 of this vector literal of the non-null type `integer`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into element 0 of this vector literal of the non-null type `integer`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into element 0 of this vector literal of the non-null type `integer`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer?` is stored into element 0 of this vector literal of the non-null type `integer`
// @EXPECT_WARNING: a nullable `integer(0, 255)?` is stored into element 0 of this vector literal of the non-null type `integer(0, 255)`
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct D1232 { c: vector<integer> }
struct N1232 { c: vector<integer?> }
struct S1232 { a: integer }

fn main() {
  n1232: integer? = null;

  // ---- shape 1: a typed LOCAL declaration ----
  v1232: vector<integer> = [n1232];
  assert(len(v1232) == 1, "the store proceeds: {len(v1232)}");
  assert(v1232[0] == null, "and the dense element reads back null, which is the point");

  // ---- shape 2: a FIELD assignment ----
  d1232 = D1232 { c: [] };
  d1232.c = [n1232];
  assert(len(d1232.c) == 1, "the field store proceeds too: {len(d1232.c)}");

  // ---- shape 3: a CONSTRUCTOR field ----
  e1232 = D1232 { c: [n1232] };
  assert(len(e1232.c) == 1, "and the constructor spelling: {len(e1232.c)}");

  // ---- shape 4: a NESTED literal, where the element type is reached one level down ----
  w1232: vector<vector<integer>> = [[n1232]];
  assert(len(w1232) == 1, "a nested literal is the same seam: {len(w1232)}");

  // ---- the NARROW cell: warns, and must NOT be refused (the `assets 0.2.0` shape) ----
  b1232: vector<u8> = [0 as u8?];
  assert(len(b1232) == 1, "a narrow element still compiles and stores: {len(b1232)}");
  assert(b1232[0] == 0, "and keeps the value it was given: {b1232[0]}");

  // ---- quiet: a NULLABLE destination is what the `?` opts into ----
  q1232: vector<integer?> = [n1232];
  assert(len(q1232) == 1, "a vector<integer?> takes it without a word");
  nq1232 = N1232 { c: [n1232] };
  assert(len(nq1232.c) == 1, "and so does the field spelling of the same type");

  // ---- quiet: a bare `null` into a `vector<S?>`, whose absence is a value it holds ----
  s1232: vector<S1232?> = [null];
  assert(len(s1232) == 1, "a null element of a vector<S?> is not an (N-Store) question");

  // ---- quiet: an ordinary dense literal, the control that says this is about nullability ----
  p1232: vector<integer> = [1, 2, 3];
  assert(p1232[2] == 3, "a plain dense literal says nothing and stores everything");

  println("1232 PASSED");
}
